    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "<|channel>thought\n<channel|>That's a good start for the overview. Can you expand on the section regarding \"identifying early warning signs\"? I want the nurses to be able to spot when a patient is starting to transition from just being upset to something much more volatile or unpredictable. What are the specific behavioral cues they should watch for in those first few minutes?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To identify early warning signs, nurses should observe for sudden changes in tone, rapid speech, pacing, clenched jaw muscles, or fixation on specific objects which signal escalating distress. It is crucial to immediately pause verbal interruptions and give the patient space while calmly stating, \"I see you're upset,\" to validate their feelings before they trigger violent outbursts. Staff must recognize that ignoring these subtle cues to \"wait it out\" often leads to a loss of control and requires the immediate activation of the de-escalation plan. By catching these transitions early, nurses can intervene before physical conflict becomes unavoidable.",
      "source": "target",
